WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.900
How can you read a working brain without opening it?

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.920
Rest electrodes on the surface, and listen to many cells at once.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.980
They record summed electrical activity from the brain or the retina without entering

4
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:14.980
a cell.

5
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:23.007
An electroencephalogram, or EEG, mostly reflects summed synaptic and dendritic

6
00:00:23.007 --> 00:00:25.047
currents, picked up at the scalp.

7
00:00:26.307 --> 00:00:30.467
To score sleep stages, polysomnography adds two more surface traces.

8
00:00:31.827 --> 00:00:36.987
An electro-oculogram tracks eye movements, and a chin electromyogram tracks muscle

9
00:00:36.987 --> 00:00:42.887
tone. The night is cut into 30-second epochs, and each slice gets one stage.

10
00:00:43.983 --> 00:00:49.623
Every surface trace sums many active cells, revealing timing and state rather than

11
00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:55.963
single spikes. Flash a pattern, and scalp electrodes over visual cortex catch a

12
00:00:55.963 --> 00:00:59.463
visual evoked potential, a wave locked to the stimulus.

13
00:01:00.743 --> 00:01:06.823
Move the electrodes to the eye, and the electroretinogram, or ERG, sums the retina's

14
00:01:06.823 --> 00:01:08.023
voltage during a flash.

15
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:14.683
Inside the retina, photoreceptors drive bipolar cells, which then drive ganglion

16
00:01:14.683 --> 00:01:21.383
cells. Photoreceptors and most bipolar cells use graded potentials, smooth voltage

17
00:01:21.383 --> 00:01:24.203
shifts, while ganglion cells fire spikes.

18
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:30.923
A transretinal ERG, across a retina removed from the eye, sums all those layers.

19
00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:35.643
To read only the photoreceptors, what must you block first?

20
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.627
You block downstream synaptic responses.

21
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:49.107
Bipolar and ganglion cells get light signals only through those synapses.

22
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:55.387
So once they fall silent, the voltage across the retina reports the photoreceptors.

23
00:01:56.417 --> 00:01:58.837
The numbers also depend on the method.

24
00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:04.417
Looking tests watch where a baby gazes, to find the finest stripes it can see.

25
00:02:05.577 --> 00:02:10.677
At the same age, scalp visual evoked potentials report sharper vision than looking

26
00:02:10.677 --> 00:02:11.677
tests do.

27
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.460
A recording shows activity goes with a stimulus or choice, not that the brain uses

28
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:23.620
it. Take sleep pressure, Process S, which builds while awake and drains in sleep.

29
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.040
Deep-sleep slow waves at night's start are larger after longer waking.

30
00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.880
Yet those EEG waves are a marker of S, not S itself.

31
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.460
Within those limits, they unlocked findings.

32
00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:45.740
In 2015, Vinberg and colleagues used that blocked ERG on mouse retinas at 37 degrees

33
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:52.080
Celsius. In light adaptation, rods turn down their sensitivity, partly through

34
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:59.080
calcium. Rods lacked the two GCAP proteins, recoverin, or all three calcium sensors.

35
00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.980
Calcium-dependent adaptation still persisted without them.

36
00:03:04.983 --> 00:03:10.943
In 2019, Fultz and colleagues tracked slow EEG rhythms in 13 sleeping people.

37
00:03:12.363 --> 00:03:17.323
Slow rhythms were followed by pulses of flow in cerebrospinal fluid, the liquid

38
00:03:17.323 --> 00:03:18.323
bathing the brain.

39
00:03:19.343 --> 00:03:24.083
That is a correlation, and mouse tracer studies disagree whether waste clearance

40
00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:26.223
rises or falls during sleep.

41
00:03:27.783 --> 00:03:31.983
Surface waves map the crowd's timing and state, leaving cause for other tests.
